<p>Ok, you seem eager for advice, so I'm gonna go over some of the reasons I downvoted. I read the containment procedures, and they're pretty good. Brief, basic, clear. So nice job. The skip then however, didn't have the same pleasure. The effect itself is mildly interesting. The type of thing that if given it's own twist could work well as an SCP, but otherwise could not. Personally, I don't really see any twist that persuaded me towards maybe upvoting.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The recovery log doesn't feel necessary. Your redactions here seem fine to me, but I'm not particularly interested in what happened because there's no real narrative or conflict.</p>
<p>The addendum-1 could be potentially interesting as an introduction into a more expansive anomaly, but it seems bogged down in the story with the breach, and this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It should be noted that both sentences Junior Researcher B█████ wrote contain all twenty-six letters of the alphabet.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Eh, I don't care all that much. It feels random and out of place. Why should this be noted? Overall, I feel like the second paragraph of addendum one was the only thing with any real value. I also disapprove of an O5 being needed to approve testing with such a basic object. It also makes me question why they were allowed to view video footage when they weren't allowed to test.</p>
<p>Addendum-2 is where expungement gets a bit out of hand for me. Potentially interesting information like what changed, and what could lead to and XK event, are simply expunged. The doctor asks permission again for some reason, even though he already has permission, and then you go into trying to destroy the object. This is something I have a big issue with. What if the object does get destroyed? Considering it's supposed to look into another universe, one with XK class scenarios in place, what happens if you break connection with this universe? The fact is, we don't know, if anything something terrible. I would never permit these tests.</p>
<p>Finally, I think the theorizations (both of them) about the parallel universes are a slight break in clinical tone. Not only that, but even without them the reader can easily make similar conclusions without it being said outright. The second time I saw the doctor's quote concerning this idea, it felt really redundant.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Anyways, my opinions. I would have no-voted if only one or two of these were present, but since I can identify things that I dislike in almost all of the portions in the skip I decided to downvote.
							</div>

								
					
