			<div class="content" id="post-content-2244818">
									

<p>Almost there, and I'd ordinarily novote, but there are multiple issues I have with this.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Feedback!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Feedback!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>First off, wording and tone. When you say stuff like:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2993 appears to be an unremarkable standard pair of aviator sunglasses with no visible markings or logos and standard durability.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Unremarkable is fine depending on how you use it; it's not good here, as it doesn't read nicely with the rest of the sentence and is padding. &quot;Appears&quot; is also something I have issue with. You wouldn't use it in this kind of context. If something is one thing but actually is another, then you'd use appears.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No form of radiation is able to pass through SCP-2993.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>No</em> form of radiation? So not even visible light?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The fact that this breaks the laws of thermodynamics is pending investigation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is silly. You don't need this sentence at all. You can let the reader figure that out on their own and imply it like you're doing. No need to state it. This also disrupts the flow of the rest of the description.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>6 hours 17 minutes and 32 seconds</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You repeat this often to the point where the article gets long to read and is tiring to see over and over. I'm not exactly sure if there's a better way to word it, but wouldn't it be better to simplify and make it &quot;six hours&quot;?</p>
<p>Secondly, your redaction and expungement. It's absolutely sloppy and drags the article down considerably. You don't need to expunge to the length you do.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2993 was recovered on ██/██/2005 in the home of Mr. ████████ ████, a ██████ Professor at [DATA EXPUNGED].</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For example, this is really unneeded. &quot;SCP-2993 was recovered on ██/██/2005 in the home of a professor at ████████ University.&quot; With that, you fixed it. The key thing here is that blackboxing dates is fine (even if people usually blackbox the year versus the month/day, but that's more of a tiny nitpick), but blackboxing the name of a person we hardly know or will care about is really not needed, and I have no clue what &quot;██████ Professor&quot; is supposed to be. And why expunge the location? Why not blackbox that out either?</p>
<p>You also repeatedly blackbox researcher and doctor names. Again, this is alright depending on the situation, but when all you can really see are blackboxes, you need to find a way to work around this. Either tell us who these people are, or limit the people you're mentioning. I recommend looking over the <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/zen-and-the-art-of-data-expunged">Zen and the Art of DATA EXPUNGED guide</a> to get a better idea of how to work at it.</p>
<p>Third, you can easily cut down on the text of a lot of this. The recovery section in particular uses a lot of words to say nothing interesting or of importance, and you do the same thing in the addenda too. All the doctors' notes don't really need to be there, especially not this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>-I am now questioning whether SCP-2993 is viewing into another universe as had been first theorized, or if, instead of showing a parallel universe, SCP-2993 shows the past of our universe in an imperfect manner with some minor differences. - Dr. ████████</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, this is something you would imply and let the reader try to figure out on their own. Don't spoonfeed it to them.</p>
<p>Finally, there's not much of a story here. True, you leave implications (even if you outright tell the reader what to assume at times) and they're interesting, but it doesn't really lead me to care much beyond that. Give the readers more, give them breadcrumbs and a story, even if it's subtle, to let them know the larger implications of this. Because otherwise, these are just sunglasses that let the user see into a parallel universe.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Feedback!</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I like this idea. I think it can be done well, and there's potential for it to be interesting. But there's too much here that isn't done well that makes me downvote.</p>

							</div>

								
