					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wrongjohnsilver" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2000367); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2000367&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609040508" alt="WrongJohnSilver" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2000367)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wrongjohnsilver" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2000367); return false;" >WrongJohnSilver</a></span> <span class="odate time_1425523138 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 Mar 2015 02:38</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2244834">
									

<p>The object has promise, but here's what I'd improve:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;You'll&nbsp;have&nbsp;20/20&nbsp;vision...</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;(puts&nbsp;on&nbsp;shades)&nbsp;...in&nbsp;hindsight.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2993 appears to be an unremarkable standard pair of aviator sunglasses with no visible markings or logos and standard durability.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No need to say &quot;unremarkable standard&quot;. Remove.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When viewed from the front, SCP-2993 is opaque and acts as a mirror&#8230;. No form of radiation is able to pass through SCP-2993. The fact that this breaks the laws of thermodynamics is pending investigation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That doesn't actually break the laws of thermodynamics. Radiation can still be absorbed and reflected just fine. It's unexpected, but not thermo-breaking. Furthermore, given that the view from the other side isn't what's being shown in front of you, then no, light isn't getting through. Remove the thermo-breaking sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2993 was recovered on ██/██/2005 in the home of Mr. ████████ ████, a ██████ Professor at [DATA EXPUNGED]. Foundation personnel were dispatched when Professor ██████ triggered multiple keywords on online forums under the pen name [DATA EXPUNGED].</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Too much expunging. So a professor said something somewhere as someone. This is too non-informative. Furthermore, he's Professor ██████ elsewhere; shouldn't he be introduced as Professor ██████ and not Mr. ████████ ████? It's clearly not the same name, by the way. All in all, the Recovery section doesn't really give us enough. The guy bought it at a yard sale. Does the fact he's a professor matter? Or that he teaches <em>something</em> at <em>something</em> U? None of that needs expunging. Or recording.</p>
<p>Similarly, no need to mention names and then expunge them. &quot;A Junior Researcher&quot; can be referred to as &quot;the researcher&quot; later in the paragraph without multiple expungements.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>APPROVED - O5-█</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Put the number there. It's okay. The O5 is still unknowable.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The notes on SCP-████ appeared to be minimally different, excluding [DATA EXPUNGED].</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is one sentence where I don't mind the censorship. It's not relevant to SCP-2993, and is in keeping with the SCP theme.</p>
<p>What I love is that it seems that first, the glasses are showing the past. Then, the glasses are showing a parallel universe. Then, the glasses are just lying. Each theory requires different responses, and the ambiguity makes them all the more interesting.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>No vote for now. It's not ready as is, but it has lots of potential with some revision.</p>

							</div>

								
					
